description:
src/backend.rs:156-172 declares:

```
/// Return the thread id of the first stored Email whose
/// `messageId` list intersects `message_ids`, or `None` if no match exists.
///
/// **Persistent backends MUST override this method.** The default `next_id`
/// generator used when this returns `None` is seeded from system-clock
/// nanoseconds at process startup. Two processes that start within the same
/// nanosecond (common in containers and test harnesses) will produce
/// identical ID sequences, silently corrupting thread graphs across
/// restarts. A persistent backend must derive thread IDs from durable
/// storage — for example, by looking up a content-addressed hash of the
/// message-id header — so that thread identity survives process boundaries.
fn find_thread_by_message_ids(
    &self,
    caller: &Self::CallerCtx,
    account_id: &jmap_types::Id,
    message_ids: &[&str],
) -> impl std::future::Future<Output = Result<Option<jmap_types::Id>, Self::Error>> + Send;
```

Two interlocking doc-contract bugs:

1. **"Persistent backends MUST override this method"** — the method has no default implementation. It is a REQUIRED method (no `{ … }` body, no default impl). Every backend MUST implement it; there is no override semantics. The "MUST override" phrasing is wrong because there is nothing to override.

2. **"The default `next_id` generator used when this returns `None`"** — this references machinery that does not appear in this trait. The "default next_id generator" lives somewhere else (likely in `MemoryBackend`'s `create_object` impl, which generates UUIDs). A consumer reading the trait doc has no way to find what the doc is talking about. The note about "process-startup-time-seeded ID generators corrupting thread graphs across restarts" is correct, but it is a property of `MemoryBackend`, not of any default behavior of this trait method.

This doc appears to have been written assuming a default implementation that delegates to a global ID generator existed at some earlier point; the method was made required at some later point but the doc was not refreshed.

Fix: rewrite the doc to describe the method's actual contract:

```
/// Look up the thread id of the first stored Email whose `messageId`
/// list intersects `message_ids`, or `None` if no match exists.
///
/// The handler uses this method during `Email/set`/`Email/import` to
/// reuse an existing thread id when an incoming message has the same
/// Message-ID / References as a stored message. If this method returns
/// `None`, the handler calls `create_object::<Email>` which generates
/// a fresh thread id via the backend's own ID generator.
///
/// **Persistence guidance**: backends with durable storage SHOULD
/// derive thread ids from a content-addressed hash of the Message-ID
/// header so that thread identity survives restarts. Backends that
/// generate thread ids from a process-startup-seeded counter (e.g.
/// `MemoryBackend`) will produce different thread graphs across
/// restarts; this is fine for tests but unacceptable for production.
```

Priority P1 because docstring promises a contract the impl does not enforce, and a careful reader will be confused enough to file a follow-up clarification bead, but a less-careful reader will skip "implementing" a method that is required.
